<PageShell title="Reflection on Assessment of Student Performance">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I view assessment as more than a mechanism for assigning grades. In my courses, assessment serves
      three related purposes: allowing students to demonstrate what they have learned, providing feedback
      about their progress, and providing me with evidence about the effectiveness of my instruction.
    </p>
    <p>
      I use multiple forms of assessment, including adaptive learning, module quizzes, laboratory work,
      application-based assignments, and cumulative examinations. This variety allows students to
      encounter and demonstrate learning in different ways rather than relying exclusively on traditional
      tests.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rubrics play an important role in my assessment practices. My application assignments are accompanied
      by detailed rubrics that communicate expectations before students begin their work. I encourage
      students to use these rubrics as planning tools rather than waiting until after grading to review
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also prioritize <a href={docLinks.timelyCommunication} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">timely communication</a> and grading. Students receive email responses within 24 hours,
      and assignment <a href={docLinks.feedbackToStudents} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">feedback</a> within 5 days of submission. I consider responsiveness an important part of
      supporting students in an accelerated course. Because an eight-week course moves quickly, delayed
      feedback can prevent students from applying what they learned to subsequent work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Student performance also informs my decisions about course design. When I observe patterns in
      student responses or receive feedback through <a href={docLinks.ideaEvaluations} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IDEA</a> evaluations, I consider whether a difficulty
      reflects a student misconception, unclear instructions, an ineffective assignment, or an area in
      which the course needs stronger instructional support.
    </p>
    <p>
      One area I have identified for continued growth is the depth of individualized feedback I provide.
      Because my rubrics are comprehensive, I have sometimes relied on the rubric to communicate where
      students did not meet expectations rather than providing sufficiently individualized comments or
      questions. Going forward, I want to move beyond identifying what was missing toward feedback that
      prompts students to reconsider their reasoning, make connections, and think more deeply about
      biological concepts.
    </p>
    <p>
      These examples will demonstrate not only what students are asked to produce but also how I use
      assessment and feedback to support their learning.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">Learning From Student Feedback</h2>

    <p>
      Student feedback has been an important source of evidence in evaluating my courses. Overall, IDEA
      evaluations indicate that students value the organization and layout of my courses, the study guides,
      the laboratory experiences, and my responsiveness to questions and concerns.
    </p>
    <p>
      These responses are particularly meaningful to me because organization and accessibility are
      intentional components of my teaching philosophy. I want students to spend their limited time
      learning Biology rather than trying to determine where materials are located or what they are
      expected to do.
    </p>
    <p>
      Student feedback has also identified areas for improvement. In one evaluation, a student reported
      difficulty locating my contact information and uncertainty about what to do during the first week of
      the course. Although this information was available in the syllabus and course announcements, the
      feedback reminded me that providing information is not necessarily the same as ensuring that students
      notice and use it. To remedy this in the future, I plan to send students a direct introductory email on
      the first day of future courses that provides my contact information and explicitly identifies the
      first steps they should take.
    </p>
    <p>
      Students have also indicated that some challenging concepts would benefit from longer instructional
      videos. My current use of shorter videos is intentional, but I recognize that not every biological
      concept can be effectively addressed within the same time constraint. I therefore plan to add longer
      instructor-created videos selectively where students would benefit from more sustained explanation.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also use student performance and observations of individual assignments to identify areas for
      improvement. For example, I am not satisfied with the current mitosis assignment, which primarily
      asks students to explain the stages of mitosis. Although this type of activity can work effectively in
      a face-to-face setting, I believe the current online version does not sufficiently promote authentic
      application and is vulnerable to AI-generated responses. I plan to replace it with a case-based
      activity that requires students to apply their understanding of cell division.
    </p>
    <p>
      These changes reflect an important part of my development as an instructor: I am increasingly willing
      to identify not only what is working, but also what I would redesign if I were developing the course
      today.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageShell>
